<BaseLayout title="GV14 (Dazhui) — Great Hammer Point" description="Everything you need to self-treat GV14 (Dazhui) — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points" }, { label: "GV14 (Dazhui)", href: "/points/gv14-dazhui-acupressure-point" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">GV14 (Dazhui) — Great Hammer</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">GV14, known as Dazhui or Great Hammer, sits below the most prominent bump on the back of the neck. It is the classic point for the early signs of a cold, fevers, headaches, neck stiffness, and supporting immunity.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Governing Vessel (GV / Du)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Back of the neck, in the hollow below the seventh cervical vertebra (C7)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">First signs of a cold, fever and chills, headache, neck stiffness, immune support and feeling the cold</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Very easy to reach yourself — it sits on the midline of the neck</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes, kneading slowly</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">2–3 times daily at the first sign of a cold</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LU7 (Lieque)</a>, <a href="/points/bl13-feishu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL13 (Feishu)</a>, <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20 (Fengchi)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent neck or cervical spine injury, or broken skin over the back of the neck (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why GV14 Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">GV14 is a point on the Governing Vessel, the meridian that runs up the midline of the back and over the head, and it sits exactly where the neck meets the shoulders, below the most prominent bump on the spine. In traditional theory it is called the meeting point of all the yang meridians — the gathering place of the body's defensive energy — which is why it has long been treated as a point for the very first signs of a cold, for chills, and for supporting the body's defenses. A fever, shivering, or the familiar "I think I'm coming down with something" feeling are exactly the states GV14 is traditionally used for.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">The point is also a natural release for the neck and upper shoulders. The C7 bump sits at the junction of the neck and back, and the muscles that attach around it tighten with desk work, poor posture, and stress. Pressing GV14 therefore does double duty — it supports the body during cold season and it loosens the stiffness that so often accompanies a headache or a long day at a screen. Because the point is on the midline, it is easy to reach with one finger and to work on yourself at any time of day, and it pairs naturally with LU7 for cold onset and BL13 for chest support.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Location</h2>
    <img src="/images/gv14-dazhui-location.webp" alt="GV14 acupressure point location below the seventh cervical vertebra" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="1061" height="837" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">GV14 sits on the back of the neck, on the midline, in the hollow just below the spinous process of the seventh cervical vertebra (C7). To find it, lower your chin toward your chest and run a finger down the back of your neck — the first prominent bump that moves as you nod is C7, the seventh cervical vertebra. The soft hollow immediately below that bump is GV14. When you lift your head, the bump tucks away, which is why the point is easiest to feel with the chin lowered.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it is the soft hollow on the midline directly below the most prominent neck bump; (2) the bump above it moves when you nod your head; (3) pressing the hollow produces a deep, achy, slightly warm sensation — never a sharp pinch on the bone.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use GV14 (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-emerald-200 bg-emerald-50 dark:border-emerald-900/40 dark:bg-emerald-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You feel the first scratchy, achy signs that a cold is coming on</li>
          <li>• You have chills, a fever, or that run-down "under the weather" feeling</li>
          <li>• Your neck is stiff where it meets the upper shoulders</li>
          <li>• You feel the cold easily and want a daily defense point in winter</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently injured the neck or cervical spine, or had whiplash or neck surgery</li>
          <li>• There is broken skin, a rash, or tenderness over the back of the neck</li>
          <li>• A stiff neck comes with a fever and headache — that needs medical attention</li>
          <li>• You feel dizzy, nauseous, or the pressure causes numbness or tingling</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: GV14 is a comfort practice for the early signs of a cold and ordinary neck stiffness, not first aid for a serious fever or a new neck injury.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Benefits &amp; Uses</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Fever and chills:</strong> GV14 is traditionally used at the start of a fever to help the body regulate temperature.</li>
      <li><strong>Colds and flu:</strong> It is the classic "first sign of a cold" point, pressed early to support recovery.</li>
      <li><strong>Headache:</strong> It may help ease headaches, especially ones that come with neck tension.</li>
      <li><strong>Neck stiffness:</strong> It relieves a stiff, tight neck where it meets the upper shoulders.</li>
      <li><strong>Immune support:</strong> As the meeting of the yang meridians, it is used to strengthen the body's defenses and combat feeling the cold.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How to Massage GV14</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Lower your chin to expose the point, then work the hollow below the C7 bump with a fingertip or knuckle. The pressure should feel deep but comfortable, never sharp against the bone.</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Lower your chin toward your chest to expose the C7 bump and the hollow below it.</li>
      <li>Place your middle fingertip or knuckle into the hollow just below the bump.</li>
      <li>Press and knead in a slow circular motion for 1–2 minutes.</li>
      <li>Gently tip your head back and forward a few times while pressing to work the area.</li>
      <li>Repeat 2–3 times a day at the first sign of a cold or when the neck feels stiff.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing on the bony bump itself or directly over the spine. The target is the soft hollow below and slightly beside the C7 bump — if you feel hard bone under your fingertip, shift down into the dip and stay there.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine GV14 with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A full cold-season routine is more effective than pressing one point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 30–60 seconds each.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">First signs of a cold</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GV14 → <a href="/points/lu7-lieque-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LU7</a> → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">LI4</a>, 30–60s each, up to 3 times on the first day</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Fever or chills</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GV14 → <a href="/points/bl13-feishu-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">BL13</a> → LI4, then rest warm and hydrate</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Headache with neck stiffness</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GV14 → <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">GB20</a> → LU7, holding each 60s for up to 3 minutes total</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Feeling the cold / immunity maintenance</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GV14 → BL13 → <a href="/points/cv6-qihai-acupressure-point" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">CV6</a> daily through cold season</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/back-acupressure-points" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">back acupressure points</a> body guide and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-neck-pain" class="text-emerald-600 hover:text-emerald-500 dark:text-emerald-400">acupressure for neck pain</a> page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The point is often tender, and the neck may feel warm or looser within a few minutes. Some people notice an easing of chills or the heavy-headed feeling early in a cold. Do not expect a cure after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With 2–3 sessions per day at the first sign of a cold, many people feel the episode stays milder and the neck stays more flexible. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for pain and respiratory comfort, where benefits build over several days of regular use.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For cold-season maintenance, keep GV14 as a daily ritual alongside sleep, warmth, and hydration. Acupressure is a support, not a treatment — if a fever is high and not settling, symptoms get worse, or a stiff neck comes with a severe headache, stop self-treatment and see a clinician.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Science &amp; Research</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for respiratory and musculoskeletal conditions has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for pain and immune-related complaints, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Points on the neck and upper back are common in studies of headache, neck pain, and immune support, and several trials report that pressure at these points may ease tension and discomfort, though the studies are small and methodologically varied.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For colds and general wellbeing, acupressure is best understood as a low-risk comfort measure that supports, but never replaces, medical care.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["lee-frazier-2011"]} note="The review above evaluated acupressure for symptom management, including immune and fever-related complaints. It is not a study of GV14 in isolation." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Precautions</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Avoid GV14 if you have a recent neck or spine injury, whiplash, or recent neck surgery.</li>
      <li>Do not press on the bony bump itself or over the top of the spine — work only the soft hollow beside and below it.</li>
      <li>Skip the point if there are skin irritations, rashes, or broken skin over the back of the neck.</li>
      <li>Stop if you feel dizzy, nauseous, or experience numbness or tingling.</li>
      <li>Seek medical care for a high fever, a stiff neck with a headache, or a fever in an infant.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>A high fever that does not settle, or a fever in an infant or young child</li>
        <li>A stiff neck together with a severe headache and fever — this can be serious</li>
        <li>Neck pain that appeared suddenly after a fall, twist, or whiplash</li>
        <li>Numbness, tingling, weakness, or pain running down the arms</li>
        <li>Cold symptoms that keep getting worse instead of improving after 1–2 weeks</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where is GV14 and how do I find it?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">GV14 is in the hollow just below the most prominent bump on the back of the neck — the seventh cervical vertebra (C7). Lower your chin and run a finger down the midline of your neck; the first big bump that moves as you nod is C7, and the soft hollow below it is GV14.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I press GV14?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Knead the hollow for 1–2 minutes at a time, 2–3 times a day at the first sign of a cold or when the neck is stiff. Tip your head gently back and forward while pressing to work the area, and stop if you feel dizzy or nauseous.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press GV14 at work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes — GV14 is on the midline of the back of the neck, so it is easy to reach discreetly at a desk. Lower your chin, press the hollow below the C7 bump with a fingertip for a minute, and tip your head gently back and forward to loosen the neck during screen breaks.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can GV14 help with neck stiffness?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. GV14 sits exactly where the neck meets the shoulders, so gentle kneading of the hollow below C7 can release tight muscles around the junction. Press for 1–2 minutes and gently tip your head back and forward while working the area.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is GV14 safe to press during a fever?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Gentle pressure at GV14 is traditionally used at the very start of a fever, but it is not a treatment for a serious illness. Rest, fluids, and your doctor's advice come first. Seek medical care for a high fever, a stiff neck with a headache, or a fever in a young child.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

